{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>On the day.</b> It is a dispute. Some say the eighth day of ordination was the first of the month of Nisan, and they needed to say that on the twenty-third of Adar Moses erected the Tabernacle, and behold on the first day of ordination Moses would dismantle it and rebuild it to train the priests. And they said that the goat which Moses sought and behold was being burned is the goat of the new moon, and behold HaShem commanded that Moses erect the Tabernacle on the first of the first month and not dismantle it until the day the Ark set out. And behold, we find in Sifrei that Mishael and Elzaphan were those impure on account of a human corpse, and behold the eighth day of ordination is the eighth of the month. And there is something like a proof for this: first, why did the verse not mention that Moses would erect the Tabernacle and dismantle it — and where would the entrance of the Tent of Meeting be after it was dismantled, for it is written \"at the entrance of the Tent of Meeting you shall sit day and night\" (Leviticus 8:35) — the verse testifies, and \"speak to the children of Israel, saying: Take a goat for a sin offering\" (Leviticus 9:3), and this is what Moses sought. And furthermore, if it was the goat of the new moon, why did he not mention two bulls and one ram and seven lambs? And when he mentioned \"besides the burnt offering of the herd\" (Numbers 28:23), it would have been fitting to say \"besides the burnt offering of the month.\" And behold, according to the plain sense, on the first day of the first month was the beginning of the erection of the Tabernacle. And behold, this first Tabernacle he erected on the first day of the first month, and they began to build the first house, which is the second Tabernacle, in the second month, which is Iyyar, on the second of the month, for thus it is written: \"in the second month, on the second\" (II Chronicles 3:2). And in our Talmud it is written that on the fifth day Israel went out from Egypt, and if it is by tradition, we accept it; and if it is by reasoning — before the fifth day they went out, for there is between a plain year and the beginning of the next year less than three days' difference, for a deficient year of 353 and 350 — they went out in the seventh [year of the cycle]; and if they are intercalated, five in deficiency. And because the verse said \"on the sabbath day he shall arrange it\" (Leviticus 24:8), it appears that on the sabbath day Moses arranged the bread on the table. And behold, on the sixth day the Tabernacle was erected, or on the sabbath — even though this is not an absolute proof, for the disputant could reply: on the sabbath day the priest shall arrange it, after the Tabernacle was erected and Moses had arranged the bread. Behold, he mentioned in the account that Moses erected the Tabernacle, as I said in the two sections, and the Levites, who are of his family, erected it, for it is not the act of an individual. And he set the sockets for the tribe, and afterward he placed the boards, and afterward the bars, and afterward he set up the pillars for the curtain and the screen of the entrance of the tent; and after that he spread the tent and placed the covering over it; and after that he placed the Ark toward the west and put the tablets in the Ark, and placed its poles on it, and put the cover on the Ark from above; and he placed the table and arranged the bread of the Presence upon it — and behold, this indicates that it was in the morning — and he said \"he lit the lamps\" afterward, at night; and he offered in the morning the burnt offering, which is the constant burnt offering, and likewise he did between the evenings. And on this day Moses anointed the Tabernacle and all its vessels, and the altar of burnt offering and all its vessels, and the laver and its base; and the assembly was gathered to the entrance of the Tent of Meeting, and Aaron and his sons washed with water; and he clothed Aaron in the holy garments; also he clothed his sons, and they were anointed with the anointing oil; and he brought a bull and two rams, and slaughtered the bull and purged the altar and burned the specified portions; and he slaughtered the ram and threw its blood on the altar and burned the ram; and he also slaughtered the ram of ordination, and threw its blood on the altar, and the specified portions with the right thigh, with one unleavened loaf and a loaf of oiled bread — all of these he burned on the altar. Thus he did all seven days of ordination, and thus it is written: \"and a bull for a sin offering you shall do for the day upon the coverings\" (Exodus 29:36), for each day, \"and you shall anoint the altar to sanctify it,\" and it is written: \"seven days you shall atone for the altar\" (Exodus 29:37), \"and you shall anoint it to sanctify it\" (Exodus 29:37). And behold, the meaning is: thus you shall do all seven days of ordination. And it is written: \"And it was on the day Moses finished erecting the Tabernacle and anointed it\" (Numbers 7:1), \"and he sanctified the altar seven days of ordination\" — then the princes began to bring near for the dedication of the altar. Thus Aaron would offer a calf for a sin offering to atone for himself, and he would also offer his ram; and a calf and a lamb, which is the offering of the people, he would offer as a burnt offering, after he slaughtered the goat of the sin offering and purged it as he did for the calf of the sin offering that was his — to throw its blood and burn the specified portions; only the sin offering of the priest, a priest may not eat — therefore the flesh of the bull was burned outside the camp; and the sin offering of the congregation, which is the goat, the priests would eat. And all of this was after the morning burnt offering. And after all this he brought the burnt offering of Nahshon and his sin offering. And behold, the dedication of the altar was completed after the nineteenth day of the first month. And do not set your heart upon the words of the disputant, as to when the festival offerings were made, for Israel did not perform at the wilderness of Sinai anything but the Pesach one night, and they did not observe the Festival of Unleavened Bread, for thus it is written: \"And it shall be when HaShem brings you\" (Exodus 13:5), \"seven days you shall eat unleavened bread\" (Exodus 12:15), and the meaning of \"and this day shall be for you a memorial\" (Exodus 12:14) is for the generation coming after, to establish the day of your going out as a sacred convocation; for on the first day they set out from Goshen. And furthermore, in the wilderness of Sinai they had no bread to make unleavened bread for seven days — even though they were near a settlement, it was a great thing that they found bread to make and to eat with bitter herbs with the Pesach for one night, for the people were very numerous. And behold, by reasoning, Moses would offer during the seven days of ordination the constant burnt offering, and on the sabbath day two lambs — whichever day fell within the seven days of ordination, he would offer the sabbath burnt offering and afterward the ordination offering. And all of this is sound, except: what was done for the dedication of the altar on the sabbath? Some say they would skip and count only the tally of the days they dedicated the altar, and others said that the burnt offering of the prince was offered on the sabbath, but the well-being offerings were slaughtered on the first day and eaten. And their proof is that behold the prince, who is the one sending, would offer on the sabbath day six lambs, besides the sabbath burnt offering of the congregation. And the first group says: thus six days they marched around, blowing, and the vanguard went — they skipped the sabbath day, and behold, seven days they encircled it. And thus they said about the fourteen days [of the dedication], that they skipped the Day of Atonement and did not include it in the count."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 40, :notes {0 "The phrase 'שנת חסרון שנ\"ג וש\"נ יצאו בשביעית' is opaque; rendered hyper-literally. The numerals שנ\"ג (353) and ש\"נ (350) refer to deficient year lengths in the Hebrew calendar. 'יצאו בשביעית' may mean the Exodus fell in the seventh year of a cycle, but the syntax is uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:41:28.766139834Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}